  "updated": "2026-09-03T01:08:59.960621+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSubsidence and loss of diurnal heating with lift/forcing well\nnorth of the region is aiding in suppressing any additional\nconvection as well as limiting further strengthening of ongoing\nconvection north of I-80. Due to these factors, the Severe\nThunderstorm Watch was cancelled for the southwestern portion of\nthe watch area in conjunction with that higher confidence in\nlittle to no severe risk.\n\nFor the rest of the evening into overnight hours, the focus is\non how far west the convective line (and its outflow) across far\nwestern NY develops; this is necessary to provide a secondary\npiece of lift to re-ignite storms with the severe chances\n(favoring damaging wind) across the rest of the Watch area.\nRadar trends suggest the line ends up staying/traversing east of\nthis forecast region, which would allow lingering storms to wane\nfor the first part of the overnight period.\n\nThough beyond the watch time frame, hi-res models are becoming\nincreasingly attached to the idea of a secondary storm cluster\ndeveloping in eastern MI (signs of this occurring is ongoing)\nand dropping towards northwest PA between 5am-10am. Even with a\nstable layer, enough elevated instability and DCAPE may be\npresent to overcome that deterrent for a damaging wind threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nStrong 594 dam ridging remains centered across the Southeast \nwith the axis of the ridge extended north through the Plains. \nLocally, we're parked beneath west-northwest mid- level flow, \nand subtle impulses in the flow combined with mesoscale surface \nfeatures will continue to be the driver of our thunderstorm \ncomplexes over the next 48 hours.\n\nConfidence in future storm complex progression decreases with \ntime in this setup as it's highly dependent on poorly modeled \npreceding rounds, but Thursday looks more favorable to see more \nwidespread coverage of storms. The mid-level ridge axis will \namplify a bit more and gradually slide east which will reorient \nthe mid-level flow more northwesterly such that we're better in \nthe path of impulses and resultant MCSs. The question is when we\nget the most coverage. Some solutions suggest morning \nconvection again similar to Wednesday morning while others don't\nand hold off until the afternoon. The latter case would result \nin more widespread, severe storms in a favorable environment \nwith a 70+% probability of 1000 J/kg SBCAPE and 30 knots of \nshear. If storms do move through in the morning, they will carry\na severe risk with them as well, there will just be more \nquestion about threats and whether or not they're surface- \nbased. Then the waters will become muddier for the afternoon, so\nwe just have to take this setup 12-24 hours at a time.\n\nCome Friday, a cold front that's been meandering well off to our \nnorth finally gets a push to move through and will put an end to the \nunsettled pattern for the weekend. More storms are possible in the \nmorning hours ahead of the front, but we should see a cessation from \nnorth to south in the second half of the day as the front passes \nthrough. It'll be very timing dependent, but there is a scenario in \nwhich most of our activity is done by mid-afternoon. Others suggest \nit lingers a bit, especially south of Pittsburgh, into the evening \nhours. The severe outlook for Friday has appropriately been pulled \nsouth and only left our area south of Pittsburgh in a marginal risk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nAside from the storms, unseasonably warm conditions continue \nthrough the end of the week. Heat risk will remain in the major\ncategory with pockets of extreme in the valleys and urban areas\nwhere temperatures reach the low to mid 90s and dew points in \nthe 70s resulting in heat indices exceeding 100F. Overnight lows\ntonight will only dip into the low 70s. Thus, the Heat Advisory\nremains in effect through Thursday.\n\nHave some lower confidence that Advisory criteria will be \nreached widespread on Thursday if convection is more widespread \nin coverage, but with how much uncertainty there is, will let \nthe headlines ride as is as areas without convective \ncontamination will heat up efficiently.",
